// src/selfhost/kel/verify_types.kel -- the self-hosted TYPE REJECTION stage.
//
// **Rejection only, and that is a measured scope rather than a simplification.**
// Two spikes shrank the Order-1 type-checker obligation to this. Monomorphization is an identity
// transform on all ten stage sources, pinned by `tests/selfhost_monomorphize_identity.rs`. And
// clearing `program.fn_expr_types` leaves every stage module byte-identical, so the emitter's
// structural fallback covers everything the subset reaches and INFERENCE FOR EMISSION IS NOT
// REQUIRED. What remains is deciding that an ill-typed program is ill-typed, and this stage needs
// exactly as much type propagation as that decision takes.
//
// **The oracle is VERDICT agreement, not message agreement.** Accept versus reject, which is what
// the `verify_*.kel` family already uses. Reproducing the reference's English would commit this
// stage to diagnostics the reference is free to reword.
//
// **The two directions are NOT symmetric, unlike the structural verifier's.** `verify_structural`
// and friends may over-approximate and defer to a runtime guard. A type checker may not: rejecting
// a valid program is a LANGUAGE CHANGE, not a conservative choice. So:
//
//   the reference accepts  ->  this stage MUST accept. A false rejection narrows the language.
//   the reference rejects  ->  this stage must reject, for the enumerated shapes.
//
// The second is bounded by a corpus; the first is not, which is why the well-typed side of the
// corpus grows with every slice rather than staying at the controls the sizing spike used.
//
// SLICES 1 to 5: operand agreement, call arity and argument types, name resolution, conditions,
// and structural claims about composites.
//
// SLICE 0, WHICH THIS REPLACED: no rules, `verdict` always ACCEPT. That is the point rather than
// a placeholder -- the corpus must then show every well-typed case agreeing and every ill-typed
// case DISAGREEING, and a harness reporting success here is broken. Finding that out costs nothing
// before a rule exists, and costs a great deal afterwards.
//
// See `docs/decisions/TYPECHECK_IMPLEMENTATION_PLAN.md` for the six slices and the fifteen shapes.
require word >= 32;

// The host-facing block. `cmd` selects the operation; `verdict` carries the answer out.
//
// The input surface is deliberately EMPTY at slice 0. The stage cannot yet see the program, and
// stating that in the encoding is better than accepting a table nothing reads: a reader of this
// file can tell at a glance that a passing corpus here proves the harness works and nothing about
// type checking.
// SLICE 1 gives the stage its first input: a table of OPERAND PAIRS, each a
// place where two values must agree in type.
//
// **The tags are SYNTACTIC, and that is what keeps the oracle honest.** They
// come from literal kinds in the parse tree -- `1` is an integer, `true` is a
// bool -- not from the reference's inference. Marshalling inferred types would
// make the stage agree with the reference by construction and prove nothing.
// An operand whose type is not syntactically evident is tag 0, UNKNOWN, and an
// unknown pair is not a rejection: this stage may not reject a valid program.
shared data ty {
    cmd: Word,
    verdict: Word,
    // Operand pairs. `n` rows of (left tag, right tag).
    n: Word,
    lhs: [Word; 256],
    rhs: [Word; 256],
    // SLICE 2: call sites, as (declared parameter count, actual argument
    // count). Arity is PURELY SYNTACTIC -- it needs no types at all -- which is
    // why it is the cheapest of the fifteen shapes to reach and why two of them
    // fall to one comparison.
    //
    // A call to a name with no declaration contributes NO row. Undefined
    // functions are slice 3, and inventing a declared count of zero for one
    // would reject it here for a reason this slice cannot defend.
    cn: Word,
    cdecl: [Word; 128],
    cact: [Word; 128],
    // SLICE 3: name resolution, as a table of per-occurrence RESOLUTION CODES.
    //
    //   1  resolves to a declared function, called as one   -- fine
    //   2  resolves to a local or parameter, read as a value -- fine
    //   3  resolves to nothing                               -- REJECT
    //   4  resolves to a local and is CALLED                 -- REJECT
    //
    // **Code 4 is not a type error.** It is a V0.2.0 surface restriction, and
    // it is the one rejection of the fifteen that carries no `type error:`
    // prefix in the reference. A stage locating rejections by that prefix would
    // miss it entirely, and a stage reproducing the reference's routing would
    // be reproducing English rather than deciding anything. It is a separate
    // code here for exactly that reason.
    rn: Word,
    res: [Word; 256],
    // SLICES 4 and 5: conditions that must be bool, and structural claims about
    // a composite. Both are "a tag must equal a required tag", so they share one
    // table of (actual, required) rather than growing a channel each.
    //
    // A required tag of 0 means "no requirement" and never rejects, which is how
    // a construct this stage cannot type stays silent rather than guessing.
    qn: Word,
    qact: [Word; 256],
    qreq: [Word; 256],
    // THE LAST TWO SHAPES: a field access naming a field its type does not
    // declare, and a body whose type differs from the declared return type.
    //
    // Membership is a table of 1/0 rather than names, because the stage compares
    // rather than searches: the host supplies the syntactic fact "this field is
    // among that type's declared fields", and the RULE -- that a referenced
    // field must exist -- is the stage's. That is the same division the
    // verify_*.kel family uses, where the host marshals op tables and the stage
    // applies the algorithm.
    mn: Word,
    member: [Word; 256],
}

// The verdict constants, named rather than spelled as bare integers at the call sites, so a later
// slice adding a rejection code cannot collide with the accept value by accident.
fn ty_accept() -> Word { 0 }

fn ty_reject() -> Word { 0 - 1 }

fn ty_unknown() -> Word { 0 }

// Two operands disagree when BOTH are known and they differ.
//
// **The unknown case accepts, and the asymmetry is the whole of the soundness
// argument.** `verify_*.kel` may over-approximate because an over-approximation
// defers to a runtime guard. A type checker has no such latitude in the
// accepting direction: rejecting a valid program is a LANGUAGE CHANGE. So an
// operand this slice cannot type is not evidence of anything, and silence is
// the only sound answer.
fn ty_pair_disagrees(l: Word, r: Word) -> Word {
    if l == ty_unknown() { 0 } else {
    if r == ty_unknown() { 0 } else {
    if l == r { 0 } else { 1 } } }
}

// A resolution code the language does not admit.
//
// Enumerated POSITIVELY rather than as "not 1 and not 2". A negative rule
// admits any code a later slice adds without anyone deciding it should, and a
// new code arriving as silently acceptable is the failure this shape is prone
// to: the codes are produced by a host walk that grows with the language.
fn ty_resolution_bad(r: Word) -> Word {
    if r == 3 { 1 } else {
    if r == 4 { 1 } else { 0 } }
}

// Slice 1: reject when any operand pair disagrees.
//
// The loop runs to completion rather than stopping at the first disagreement,
// because the language is total and there is nothing to break out of. The
// verdict is sticky, which also means a second disagreement cannot un-reject a
// program.
fn ty_check() -> Word {
    ty.verdict = ty_accept();
    for i in 0..ty.n limit 256 {
        ty.verdict = if ty_pair_disagrees(ty.lhs[i], ty.rhs[i]) == 1 {
            ty_reject()
        } else {
            ty.verdict
        };
    }
    // Slice 2. Arity is exact rather than a bound: a call with too FEW and one
    // with too MANY arguments are both rejections, and they are the same
    // comparison rather than two rules.
    for j in 0..ty.cn limit 128 {
        ty.verdict = if ty.cdecl[j] == ty.cact[j] { ty.verdict } else { ty_reject() };
    }
    // Slice 3. Unresolved names and called locals.
    for k in 0..ty.rn limit 256 {
        ty.verdict = if ty_resolution_bad(ty.res[k]) == 1 { ty_reject() } else { ty.verdict };
    }
    // Slices 4 and 5. A required tag of 0 is no requirement, so it reuses the
    // same disagreement predicate the operand pairs use: unknown never rejects.
    for q in 0..ty.qn limit 256 {
        ty.verdict = if ty_pair_disagrees(ty.qact[q], ty.qreq[q]) == 1 {
            ty_reject()
        } else {
            ty.verdict
        };
    }
    // A referenced field must be declared. Only 0 rejects: a row the host could
    // not decide is not written at all, so absence and denial stay distinct.
    for m in 0..ty.mn limit 256 {
        ty.verdict = if ty.member[m] == 0 { ty_reject() } else { ty.verdict };
    }
    ty.verdict
}

fn main(cmd: Word) -> Word {
    if cmd == 0 { ty_check() } else { 0 - 99 }
}
